Initiate key updates as quic-go does.

quic-go asks shouldInitiateKeyUpdate for every packet it seals and rolls its keys first when it says
so (vendor/quic-go/internal/handshake/updatable_aead.go:318-356): once the handshake is confirmed and
100 packets have been sent or opened with the first keys (FirstKeyUpdateInterval, :31-32), and then
whenever 100000 have been with the current ones (KeyUpdateInterval,
vendor/quic-go/internal/protocol/params.go:151) — a later update only once a packet sent with the
current keys has been acknowledged (updateAllowed, :306-316). Its counters restart at every roll,
the peer's included, and a packet opened with the peer's new keys is not counted (:224-258).
ngtcp2 initiates only when the application asks, or at the AEAD limit.

The counts are ngtcp2's own use_count on the send key and a new lc_rcvd on the receive key, the
phase a new lc_phase bumped by conn_rotate_keys; conn_lc_key_update runs where ngtcp2 prepares its
next keys before each write. Keys not yet prepared, or the previous receive key not yet dropped, put
the update off to a later packet — which at these counts is only ever the first one after them.

The key phase bit sits under header protection, so this is visible to the node and to nobody on
the path; it is the reference's behaviour all the same.

